ical uncertainty affecting  the economy in ways that we cannot yet  see so I'll start by talking about that  rather specifically in the context of  Britain over the over the last few years  and in the years ahead because that's  obviously been a big theme of our  political discourse our since June 2016  the referendum the claim is that nothing  has really changed as a result of the  brexit referendum of the fact that  Britain is almost certainly going to be  leaving the European Union and therefore  all the predictions that were made  during the referendum campaign by those  those people I would say I was going to  say those of us so let me repeal reveal  to you exactly where I stand are all the  predictions that were made by those of  us who believe that it was a serious  mistake a very bad mistake to leave the  European Union could be completely  dismissed as a some kind of fantastic  project fear that is a classic example  of what was described in the rubric the  economics of uncertainty it is not the  economic results are not yet visible or  not yet fully visible to the naked eye  if you like to the general public of  something that has happened now the  evidence is actually if you look at it  closely and if you're an economist is  pretty clear that already  even though brexit hasn't happened  already the anticipation of brexit has  actually had a very significant effect  on the British economy and in fact  strangely enough that effect almost  uncannily in line with the predictions  that were made during the project fifth  period and according to most members of  our government and probably our future  prime minister have been totally and  utterly contradicted by events the fact  is that the predictions that were made  and I've got a few charts just  just lighten us up the main prediction  that was made before the brexit  referendum and the scariest one if you  like was that the average family the  mean family in in the UK would lose  something that mean household would lose  something like 4,500 to 5,000 pounds in  purchasing power per year as a result of  the most extreme form of rec said namely  the total rupture with Europe and  membership under WTO rules which is now  pretty much the official policy not just  of the extreme of the Conservative Party  but of all the leadership contenders in  the Conservative Party and that is the  prediction that is said to have been  totally refuted by events now there was  a second prediction which was refuted  which was the idea that there would be a  recession immediately after the brexit  vote now that didn't happen there was a  big fall in certain parts of the country  there was really a collapse and for  example in property prices especially in  London Southeast about as big as the one  that occurred in 2008-2009 the financial  crisis but there wasn't a generalised  recession ah and that was a prediction  crucially that was only made by the Bank  of England on the basis of short-term  economic forecasting which has never  worked and incidentally economic  forecasters have never correctly  predicted recession over the last 45  years  the IMF did a study they identified  something like 350 recessions  identifiable recessions around the world  in different countries and they found  that there was not a single case in  which the the conventional economic  forecasts used by central bankers for  predicting what's going on in the next 6  to 12 months predicted that recession as  as much as a year ahead so the fact that  they failed to predict a recession is  not surprising it's absolutely it's  absolutely part par for the course but  the really significant are a forecast  that were made before before the  referendum was this idea that our living  standards would  all dramatically by as I say 4,500 or as  much as 5,000 pounds per household as a  result of the most extreme form of  Rexton WTO membership now you'll say of  course that hasn't happened you know  maybe living standards have 